When a spherical soap bubble lands on a horizontal wet surface and forms a hemisphere of the same volume, the radius of the hemisphere is equal to the radius of the original bubble multiplied by \sqrt{2}.

\begin{align}
    r_h = r_b \sqrt{2}
\end{align}

Given radius of hemisphere $r_h = 3\sqrt[3]{2}$ cm, we can solve for radius of the original bubble as follows:

\begin{align}
    r_h &= r_b \sqrt{2}\\
    3\sqrt[3]{2} &= r_b \sqrt{2}
\end{align}

Solving for $r_b$, we get:

\begin{align}
    r_b &= \frac{3\sqrt[3]{2}}{\sqrt{2}}\\
    r_b &= \frac{3\sqrt[3]{2}}{\sqrt{2}} \times \frac{\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{2}}\\
    r_b &= \frac{3\sqrt[3]{2}\sqrt{2}}{2}\\
    r_b &= \frac{3 \cdot 2^{\frac{1}{3}} \cdot 2^{\frac{1}{2}}}{2}\\
    r_b &= \frac{3 \cdot 2^{\frac{5}{6}}}{2}\\
    r_b &= \frac{3}{2} \cdot 2^{\frac{5}{6}}\\
    r_b &= \frac{3}{2^{1-\frac{5}{6}}}\\
    r_b &= \frac{3}{2^{\frac{1}{6}}}\\
    r_b &= 3 \cdot 2^{-\frac{1}{6}}\\
    r_b &= 3 \cdot 2^{-\frac{1}{6}} \cdot \frac{2^{\frac{1}{6}}}{2^{\frac{1}{6}}}\\
    r_b &= \frac{3 \cdot 2^{\frac{1}{6}}}{2^{\frac{1}{6}}}\\
    r_b &= 3
\end{align}
